======================  WHY PAST ATTEMPTS FAILED (STRUCTURE) ======================
Judges reject drawings where the frame is not a single closed loop, the fork/handlebar does
not visibly touch the front wheel, or there is no chain/chainring/crank. This happens when the
structural code below gets abbreviated or skipped. To prevent that: the five numbered blocks
below are not optional suggestions — copy them into your function nearly verbatim (you may
change colours, weights, and add wiggle/wash on top, but every coordinate call listed must
appear in your output, unmodified, so the shapes are guaranteed to connect).

======================  MANDATORY STRUCTURE (use these numbers exactly)  ======================
Define once at the top of paint():
  const R = 95;
  const RH = [-120, 90];   // rear hub
  const FH = [120, 90];    // front hub, |FH.x-RH.x| = 240, same y as RH → equal wheels, level ground
  const BB = [-40, 90];    // bottom bracket, on the ground line between the hubs
  const ST = [-90, -60];   // top of seat tube
  const HT = [95, -40];    // top of head tube / fork, above front hub
  const HB = [60, -90];    // handlebar grip, above and behind head top

Draw in this exact order. Do not omit any block.

1. WHEELS — noField so they stay perfectly circular/concentric:
   brush.noField();
   brush.circle(RH[0], RH[1], R, false);
   brush.circle(FH[0], FH[1], R, false);
   brush.circle(RH[0], RH[1], 6, false);
   brush.circle(FH[0], FH[1], 6, false);
   // optional: 3-4 brush.line spokes per wheel from hub to rim

2. CLOSED FRAME — one loop that returns to its start, ink brush, e.g.:
   brush.set('pen', INK, 2.5);
   brush.polygon([RH, BB, HT, ST, RH]);
   // this single call MUST be present: it is what makes the frame "closed" to the judge.

3. FORK + HANDLEBAR — the fork line MUST end exactly at FH (front hub), no gap:
   brush.line(HT[0], HT[1], FH[0], FH[1]);          // fork: head tube -> front hub
   brush.line(HT[0], HT[1], HB[0], HB[1]);          // steering tube -> handlebar
   brush.line(HB[0]-15, HB[1]-6, HB[0]+15, HB[1]+6); // handlebar grip, perpendicular-ish

4. DRIVETRAIN — chainring at BB plus TWO chain lines reaching RH, plus a crank:
   brush.circle(BB[0], BB[1], 24, false);
   brush.line(BB[0]+22, BB[1]-4, RH[0]+18, RH[1]-4);
   brush.line(BB[0]+22, BB[1]+4, RH[0]+18, RH[1]+4);
   brush.line(BB[0], BB[1], BB[0]+18, BB[1]+18);

5. SEAT + WASHES: a small saddle shape at ST, then go back over the whole bicycle with one or
   two translucent colour washes (brush.fill + brush.polygon/circle behind the frame and
   wheels, then brush.noFill()) so the linework reads as painted. Use brush.wiggle(0.15-0.3)
   on some ink lines for a hand-drawn feel afterward, but NEVER wiggle or field-warp the wheel
   circles, hubs, or the fork/frame connection points — they must stay exact and connected.
   There is no brush.noWiggle(); if you called brush.wiggle earlier and want straight lines
   again, just don't call wiggle before that shape, or call brush.wiggle(0) instead.

Choose two or three saturated colours of your own for the washes and keep them consistent.
Ink ALL structural linework (wheels, frame polygon, fork, handlebar, chain, crank) in one
near-black colour so the judge can trace every mechanical connection clearly. Do the washes
either fully before step 1 or fully after step 5 — never let a fill/noFill pair interrupt
the structural line sequence above.

