======================  MANDATORY SKELETON — COPY THIS STRUCTURE, DO NOT WEAKEN IT  ======================
The judge checks five things: (1) two equal-radius wheels on one ground line, (2) one closed
frame polygon touching both hubs, (3) a fork line from the head tube straight into the front
hub plus a handlebar, (4) a chainring linked by two chain lines to the rear hub, (5) it
reads as a painted bicycle, not a bare outline. Past attempts failed because they skipped or
rewrote this geometry, or forgot to declare a coordinate object before using it.

Build paint() around EXACTLY this skeleton. You MAY change the wash colours, add extra
wiggle/hatch/texture calls, add a saddle shape, or add more spokes — but you MUST NOT
delete, rename, or reorder any of the const declarations or the core structural brush
calls shown here (wheels, frame polygon, fork, handlebar, chainring, chain lines):

  const R  = 95;
  const RH = {x:-120, y:90};   // rear hub
  const FH = {x: 120, y:90};   // front hub  (same y as RH → equal ground line)
  const BB = {x: -40, y:90};   // bottom bracket
  const ST = {x: -90, y:-60};  // seat top
  const HT = {x:  95, y:-40};  // head tube top
  const HB = {x:  60, y:-90};  // handlebar grip

  const INK   = '#2b2118';
  const WASH1 = '#3a6b7a';   // choose your own saturated hues, keep them consistent
  const WASH2 = '#c1502e';

  // --- washes first (translucent, behind the linework) ---
  brush.fill(WASH1, 90);
  brush.fillBleed(0.15, 'out');
  brush.fillTexture(0.6, 0.4);
  brush.circle(RH.x, RH.y, R + 10, false);
  brush.circle(FH.x, FH.y, R + 10, false);
  brush.noFill();

  brush.fill(WASH2, 70);
  brush.polygon([[RH.x,RH.y],[BB.x,BB.y],[HT.x,HT.y],[ST.x,ST.y]]);
  brush.noFill();

  // --- ink structural linework ---
  brush.set('rotring', INK, 2);
  brush.noField();               // keep wheels perfectly circular

  // 1. WHEELS (identical radius R)
  brush.circle(RH.x, RH.y, R, false);
  brush.circle(FH.x, FH.y, R, false);
  brush.circle(RH.x, RH.y, 6, false);
  brush.circle(FH.x, FH.y, 6, false);
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    const a = i * Math.PI / 2;
    brush.line(RH.x, RH.y, RH.x + R*Math.cos(a), RH.y + R*Math.sin(a));
    brush.line(FH.x, FH.y, FH.x + R*Math.cos(a), FH.y + R*Math.sin(a));
  }

  // 2. CLOSED FRAME — one polygon touching RH, BB, HT, ST and back to RH
  brush.polygon([[RH.x,RH.y],[BB.x,BB.y],[HT.x,HT.y],[ST.x,ST.y],[RH.x,RH.y]]);

  // 3. FORK + STEERING — fork MUST touch FH exactly
  brush.line(HT.x, HT.y, FH.x, FH.y);
  brush.line(HT.x, HT.y, HB.x, HB.y);
  brush.line(HB.x - 15, HB.y, HB.x + 15, HB.y);

  // 4. DRIVETRAIN — chainring at BB, chain to RH, crank
  brush.circle(BB.x, BB.y, 24, false);
  brush.line(BB.x + 22, BB.y - 4, RH.x + 18, RH.y - 4);
  brush.line(BB.x + 22, BB.y + 4, RH.x + 18, RH.y + 4);
  brush.line(BB.x, BB.y, BB.x + 18, BB.y + 18);

  // 5. SEAT + finishing touches
  brush.line(ST.x - 10, ST.y - 8, ST.x + 14, ST.y - 8);
  brush.field('hand');
  brush.wiggle(0.2);
  brush.noField();

You may add optional decorative strokes (extra spokes, a second wash pass with
brush.hatch, ground-line texture, etc.) AFTER the block above, but every one of the
numbered structural calls above must remain present, unmodified in coordinates, and in
this relative order. Do not replace any circle with a blob, do not leave the frame
polygon open, do not omit the fork-to-FH line or the two chain lines.

