======================  MANDATORY STRUCTURE (use these numbers)  ======================
Define once at the top of paint():
  const R = 95;                      // wheel radius, identical for both wheels
  const RH = [-120, 90];             // rear hub
  const FH = [ 120, 90];             // front hub  (|FH[0]-RH[0]| ~ 240, same y → equal wheels, shared ground line)
  const BB = [ -40, 90];             // bottom bracket / pedal axle, on the ground line between the hubs
  const ST = [ -90, -60];            // top of seat tube
  const HT = [  95, -40];            // top of head tube / fork, above front hub
  const HB = [  60, -90];            // handlebar grip, above and behind head top

Draw, in this order:

1. WHEELS (identical radius R, drawn with brush.noField() so they stay circular/concentric):
   brush.noField();
   brush.circle(RH[0], RH[1], R, false);   brush.circle(FH[0], FH[1], R, false);   // rims
   brush.circle(RH[0], RH[1], 6, false);   brush.circle(FH[0], FH[1], 6, false);   // hubs
   Optionally add 3-4 straight brush.line() spokes per wheel from hub to rim.

2. CLOSED FRAME — one continuous closed polygon touching RH, BB, ST, HT and back to RH:
   brush.polygon([RH, BB, HT, ST, RH]);
   drawn as ink lines so every vertex connects to the next with no gaps — this is the
   "closed, weldable frame".

3. FORK + STEERING: brush.line(HT[0], HT[1], FH[0], FH[1]) — the fork, joining the head
   tube directly to the FRONT hub (must touch FH exactly). Then brush.line(HT[0], HT[1],
   HB[0], HB[1]) up to the handlebar, plus a short perpendicular brush.line at HB for
   the grip.

4. DRIVETRAIN: brush.circle(BB[0], BB[1], 24, false) — the chainring, centred on BB.
   Then draw the chain as two roughly-parallel lines from the chainring's edge to the
   rear hub RH, e.g.
   brush.line(BB[0]+22, BB[1]-4, RH[0]+18, RH[1]-4);
   brush.line(BB[0]+22, BB[1]+4, RH[0]+18, RH[1]+4);
   A pedal/crank: brush.line(BB[0], BB[1], BB[0]+18, BB[1]+18).

5. SEAT + WASHES: put a small saddle shape at ST, then go back over the whole bicycle with
   one or two translucent colour washes (brush.fill + brush.polygon/circle behind the
   frame and wheels, then brush.noFill() afterwards) so the linework reads as painted,
   not just outlined. Use brush.wiggle(0.15-0.3) on some ink lines for a hand-drawn feel
   (there is no brush.noWiggle — to stop wiggling, simply call brush.wiggle(0) or avoid
   calling brush.wiggle before drawing lines that must stay clean), but NEVER wiggle or
   field-warp the wheel circles or hub points — they must stay two clean, equal,
   concentric circles (call brush.noField() again if a field was set since step 1).

